# Summary
Introduced: the new refresh merge preserves historical routes indefinitely and adds the previous current route set on every epoch advance without any pruning policy.
The new StorageNodeProcessConfig::from_runtime_map_refresh method is intended to merge the bounded control-plane delta with locally retained history. However, it copies every existing historical route with an epoch older than the next epoch, adds every current route when advancing epochs, and then adds the new response history. There is no limit, history floor check, or consultation of the node's actual cluster-map history reference summary to discard routes that are no longer referenced by local shard/backfill state. As a result, a long-running node that experiences many cluster epoch changes can accumulate one historical route per PG per epoch indefinitely in its in-memory process config. This partially defeats the intent of bounding refresh history and can cause increasing memory/CPU overhead because configs are cloned and installed during refreshes. The issue appears to be an internal availability/correctness bug rather than an externally exploitable S3 security vulnerability.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit `84eb8013 Bound storage-node refresh history retention`.

The storage-node runtime-map refresh merge now takes the node's `PgClusterMapHistoryReferenceSummary` from the same heartbeat used for the control-plane refresh. After merging local retained history, the previous current route set, and the bounded control-plane delta, it prunes historical routes by the actual local retention requirement:

- if the node has no durable route-history reference, only the previous current route set is retained for refresh continuity;
- if the node has a durable reference floor, routes at or after that floor are retained, plus the latest predecessor route per PG so change-point reconstruction at the floor remains valid;
- exact metadata-transfer source-route epochs from the control-plane refresh are protected separately, so in-progress metadata-transfer retries keep the historical peering route required by storage-node transfer inspection.

The fix added regressions for repeated epoch churn with bounded retained history, retained-floor predecessor preservation, and metadata-transfer source-route retention. The storage-node server test group passed with these cases (`cargo nextest run -p storage storage_node_server::tests`), and `cargo check -p storage` passed.

