## Report
Validated the finding as a reproducible storage-node refresh history growth bug, not a memory-safety crash. Key code evidence: crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:408-435 implements StorageNodeProcessConfig::from_runtime_map_refresh. It copies every current.historical_pg_routes entry older than next.cluster_epoch at lines 420-424, adds every current pg_route when current.cluster_epoch < next.cluster_epoch at lines 425-429, then adds next.historical_pg_routes at lines 430-434, and installs the merged values without a size bound or floor-based pruning at line 435. Runtime refresh uses this helper at crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:1143-1145. The only subsequent validation at crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:461-489 checks node_id/data_dir/ec_shape/socket_path and does not prune history. The control plane side does bound the response by observed epoch: crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:747-751 computes response_floor from observed_epoch, and lines 753-763 include only history at or above that floor. I added a targeted integration PoC at crates/storage/tests/refresh_history_growth.rs. It advances the cluster epoch 8 times while requesting each storage-node refresh from the node's immediately previous observed epoch. The control-plane delta remains bounded at delta_history=1 each time, but StorageNodeProcessConfig::from_runtime_map_refresh retains one route for every previous epoch. Cargo test output: step=1 retained_history=1 ... step=8 retained_history=8; final_retained_history=8 retained_epochs=[4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11]. This confirms linear unbounded accumulation across refreshes despite bounded deltas. Direct crash was attempted via the PoC and no crash occurred, which is expected for this availability/resource-growth issue. Valgrind was unavailable in the container, and Rust ASan could not be enabled because the installed stable toolchain rejects -Z sanitizer=address. I also ran the PoC under LLDB non-interactively; it reproduced the same growth and exited normally.

